# Forma is bring-your-own-key. There is deliberately NO application API key.
#
# Each user supplies their own Gemini key in the app's Settings dialog, and the
# browser calls Google directly with it. Nothing here reaches the client bundle:
# vite.config.ts allowlists VITE_* only, so a GEMINI_API_KEY set in this file
# would be ignored by the browser rather than inlined into the shipped JS.
#
# That allowlist exists because an earlier build did inline the key, and Google's
# secret scanner revoked it (403 "Your API key was reported as leaked"). Do not
# add a Gemini key here expecting the app to pick it up — it will not, by design.

# VITE_FORMA_MOCK: set to "true" to skip Gemini entirely and return the canned
# mock invoice after a 3s delay. Local development only, for exercising loaders
# and progress bars without spending a request. Leave unset otherwise.
# VITE_FORMA_MOCK="true"

# VITE_FORMA_FLAT: set to "true" to go back to the single page-sized DetailBand
# the prompt used until 2026-09-03, instead of the real band skeleton --
# ReportHeader / PageHeader / a one-row Detail / ReportFooter / PageFooter -- it
# now asks for. A fallback, not an experiment.
#
# The flat shape produces a faithful picture and a report that cannot be bound to
# data: a DetailBand prints once per record, so one the height of the page makes
# the whole page repeat per row. Banded fixes that at the cost of a coordinate
# subtraction the model has to perform per band. Flat is the shape with a real
# designer check behind it, so this is the one-variable way back if a document
# comes out worse. See src/lib/reportBands.ts.
# VITE_FORMA_FLAT="true"

# VITE_FORMA_BIND was removed on 2026-09-05. It bound the detail row at
# generation time to field names DERIVED from the column headings -- the report
# guessing at its own schema -- and against a source whose column is really
# DESCR that produces a file which opens cleanly and fails when it runs.
#
# The Data tab does the same job properly: paste a JSON row, a CSV export, a
# CREATE TABLE or a list of column names, correct the suggested mapping, and
# apply. The names then come from the data instead of from the document, and
# the footer totals use the same mapping rather than deriving their own. There
# is nothing to configure, so this variable no longer exists -- setting it does
# nothing. This comment is here rather than nothing, for the same reason the
# APP_URL note below its removal is: a variable someone remembers and cannot
# find is worse than one explained.

# PORT: which port the server listens on. Defaults to 3000. A value that is not
# a valid port number is ignored rather than obeyed.
# PORT="3000"

# HOST: which interface the server listens on. Unset means loopback in
# development and every interface in production, which is what you want. Set it
# to 0.0.0.0 only to reach the dev server from another device on your network,
# and remember that publishes Vite's module graph to anything that can reach the
# machine.
# HOST="0.0.0.0"

# HTTPS: set to "true" only where TLS actually terminates, so the server sends
# HSTS. Do NOT set it locally -- a browser that receives HSTS on http://localhost
# refuses plain-HTTP localhost for a year afterwards, breaking every other
# project on the machine.
# HTTPS="true"

# APP_URL was here until 2026-08-27 and was read by nothing (audit INV-006). It
# was presented as required configuration and had no effect whatever: no source
# file referenced it, and index.html names it only to explain why og:image is
# root-relative *instead* of using it. Removed rather than documented as
# reserved, because a variable nobody reads is one someone eventually sets and
# then spends twenty minutes wondering about.
